[0.45, "o", "check[ok]: host:generic-linux:ssh-auth (required=false) - SSH-managed route 'ssh-managed-remote' targets host 'generic-linux' (provider 'generic-linux') via ubuntu@builder.example.internal:2222 with lifecycle owner 'ssh-remote-port-runtime'. Supply SSH auth material through the operator SSH environment; this lane does not use hosted control-plane bearer tokens.\r\ncheck[fail]: host:generic-linux:ssh-bootstrap (required=false) - SSH-managed route 'ssh-managed-remote' expects inventory owner 'ssh-remote-runtime' and lifecycle owner 'ssh-remote-port-runtime' on host 'generic-linux' (provider 'generic-linux'). Remote bootstrap must install the Linux execution prerequisites and Port runtime on the remote host; the first SSH lifecycle slice now covers launch, status, and stop, but Port still will not fall back to local runtime or hosted control-plane ownership for the remaining SSH workflows.\r\nnote: port doctor reports the host state without mutating runtime directories.\r\nnote: macOS operators can run Port against the AVF lane locally; Firecracker and Firecracker/PVM still require Linux host access.\r\nnote: Windows operators should use WSL or a remote Linux host, then rely on port doctor to confirm whether local Firecracker launch is available.\r\nnote: SSH-managed remote hosts surface separate auth and bootstrap expectations; they do not reuse hosted control-plane bearer-token auth or local runtime ownership.\r\nnote: Remote Linux hosts are modeled provider-by-provider, but the MVP launch path is still local Linux only.\r\nnote: Firecracker/PVM readiness is reported as a dedicated host-kit lane; failing PVM checks do not imply the standard Firecracker lane is a compatible fallback.\r\nnote: Cloud Hypervisor readiness is reported through its own host-platform, architecture, protection-mode, and binary checks; Port does not silently fall back to Firecracker when that lane is selected.\r\nnote: AVF install and help surfaces stay on the canonical port CLI: set PORT_AVF_LAUNCHER to an external launcher helper for local macOS workflows, and do not expect a bundled macOS-only fallback workflow in this slice.\r\nrun-parts: unrecognized option '--regex'\r\nBusyBox v1.37.0 () multi-call binary.\r\n\r\nUsage: run-parts [-a ARG]... [-u UMASK] [--reverse] [--test] [--exit-on-error] [--list] DIRECTORY\r\n\r\nRun a bunch of scripts in DIRECTORY\r\n\r\n\t-a ARG\t\tPass ARG as argument to scripts\r\n\t-u UMASK\tSet UMASK before running scripts\r\n\t--reverse\tReverse execution order\r\n\t--test\t\tDry run\r\n\t--exit-on-error\tExit if a script exits with non-zero\r\n\t--list\t\tPrint names of matching files even if they are not executable\r\n"]
